", "lat": 44.2809, "lon": -68.2472}, {"n": "Mount Desert Narrows", "u": 0.35513, "v": 0.29213, "k": "water", "lat": 44.4415, "lon": -68.337}, {"n": "Eagle Lake", "u": 0.50056, "v": 0.53693, "k": "water", "lat": 44.3635, "lon": -68.2502}, {"n": "Jordan Pond", "u": 0.492, "v": 0.63764, "k": "water", "lat": 44.3314, "lon": -68.2553}, {"n": "Echo Lake", "u": 0.35435, "v": 0.64883, "k": "water", "lat": 44.3278, "lon": -68.3373}, {"n": "Long Pond", "u": 0.31021, "v": 0.64217, "k": "water", "lat": 44.3299, "lon": -68.3636}, {"n": "Seal Cove Pond", "u": 0.25419, "v": 0.73271, "k": "water", "lat": 44.301, "lon": -68.3969}, {"n": "Bubble Pond", "u": 0.51936, "v": 0.59623, "k": "water", "lat": 44.3446, "lon": -68.239}, {"n": "The Tarn", "u": 0.57506, "v": 0.56075, "k": "water", "lat": 44.3559, "lon": -68.2058}, {"n": "Mount Desert Island", "u": 0.34282, "v": 0.5823, "k": "region", "lat": 44.349, "lon": -68.3442}, {"n": "Schoodic Peninsula", "u": 0.82282, "v": 0.52639, "k": "region", "lat": 44.3667, "lon": -68.0581}, {"n": "Western Mountain", "u": 0.29551, "v": 0.7281, "k": "region", "lat": 44.3025, "lon": -68.3723}, {"n": "The Bubbles", "u": 0.49099, "v": 0.59748, "k": "region", "lat": 44.3442, "lon": -68.2559}, {"n": "Amphitheater Valley", "u": 0.46598, "v": 0.66367, "k": "region", "lat": 44.3231, "lon": -68.2708}, {"n": "Sieur de Monts Spring", "u": 0.57052, "v": 0.5435, "k": "region", "lat": 44.3614, "lon": -68.2085}, {"n": "Sand Beach", "u": 0.61555, "v": 0.64448, "k": "region", "lat": 44.3292, "lon": -68.1817}, {"n": "Thunder Hole", "u": 0.60532, "v": 0.67209, "k": "region", "lat": 44.3204, "lon": -68.1878}, {"n": "Otter Cliff", "u": 0.60349, "v": 0.70284, "k": "region", "lat": 44.3106, "lon": -68.1889}, {"n": "Schoodic Point", "u": 0.83846, "v": 0.63959, "k": "region", "lat": 44.3306, "lon": -68.0489}, {"n": "The Ovens", "u": 0.4766, "v": 0.30352, "k": "region", "lat": 44.4379, "lon": -68.2645}], "tours": [{"id": "frenchman", "name": "Frenchman Bay", "blurb": "The cottage town, the granite islands off its front door, and the bar that names it \u2014 all drawn in 1904, when the summer people still came by steamer.", "keys": [{"lat": 44.3876, "lon": -68.2039, "d": 6, "az": 250, "el": 15, "cap": "Bar Harbor, 1904. The village grid runs down to the shore and stops; the scattered rectangles along the coast beyond it are the cottage colony. Look north across the bay: a dashed line lettered MOUNT DESERT FERRY runs to Hancock Point, where the Maine Central met the Bar Harbor steamers from 1884 until 1931."}, {"lat": 44.4001, "lon": -68.1828, "d": 7, "az": 210, "el": 14, "cap": "The Porcupines \u2014 Bald, Sheep, Burnt and Long, four granite loaves in a row. The 1904 plate draws every one with contours; the 1942 sheet behind it adds the depth curves around them."}, {"lat": 44.399, "lon": -68.2036, "d": 3.5, "az": 200, "el": 13, "cap": "Bar Island, and the bar. Twice a day about eleven feet of tide drains off a gravel spit and you can walk to the island from the town pier \u2014 that crossing is what the harbour, and then the town, was named for."}, {"lat": 44.354, "lon": -68.1372, "d": 8, "az": 290, "el": 13, "cap": "Egg Rock, four miles out, lit since 1875 \u2014 the last thing a Boston boat passed before turning in for the Bar Harbor wharf."}]}, {"id": "green", "name": "Green Mountain", "blurb": "The highest ground on the Atlantic coast of the United States \u2014 with a cog railway up it, a name change in 1918, and a motor road by 1931.", "keys": [{"lat": 44.3513, "lon": -68.2265, "d": 7, "az": 100, "el": 16, "cap": "Cadillac Mountain, 1,530 feet \u2014 the highest headland on the American Atlantic. On the 1904 plate under an earlier printing it is Green Mountain; the U.S. Geographic Board renamed it in 1918 for Antoine Laumet, sieur de Cadillac, granted this island in 1688."}, {"lat": 44.3635, "lon": -68.2502, "d": 6, "az": 120, "el": 14, "cap": "Eagle Lake, foot of the Green Mountain Cog Railway. From 1883 to 1890 a cog engine pushed a single car up the west slope to a summit hotel; the line never paid, shut down, and was scrapped. Both editions draw the grade \u2014 a dead-straight line up an otherwise trackless mountainside."}, {"lat": 44.351, "lon": -68.1942, "d": 6, "az": 60, "el": 15, "cap": "Champlain Mountain, 1,058 feet, dropping straight into Frenchman Bay. It was Newport Mountain until the same 1918 renaming; Dry Mountain became Dorr, for the man who spent his fortune assembling the park."}, {"lat": 44.3446, "lon": -68.239, "d": 5, "az": 190, "el": 14, "cap": "Bubble Pond, in the notch between Cadillac and Pemetic \u2014 a glacier ran south down this trough and left the pond in its floor. Pull the slider to 1942 and Rockefeller's carriage road is drawn along its east shore."}]}, {"id": "sound", "name": "Somes Sound", "blurb": "Five miles of salt water cut into the middle of the island \u2014 the settlement at its head, and the granite quarried out of its side.", "keys": [{"lat": 44.31, "lon": -68.3099, "d": 9, "az": 350, "el": 13, "cap": "Somes Sound, the salt inlet that nearly halves the island. Ice cut it, not a river: a glacier followed a line of weak rock and the sea came in behind. It runs better than 150 feet deep inside a shallow sill at the mouth \u2014 the reason it gets called a fjord."}, {"lat": 44.3623, "lon": -68.3342, "d": 5, "az": 170, "el": 13, "cap": "Somesville, at the head of the sound. Abraham Somes brought his family here in 1761 \u2014 the first permanent English settlement on the island, and for a century its mill town, sawing lumber at the outlet of Somes Pond."}, {"lat": 44.3359, "lon": -68.3214, "d": 4, "az": 280, "el": 13, "cap": "Hall Quarry, cut into the sound's west wall. Mount Desert granite went out of here by schooner from the 1870s \u2014 the island's real industry while Bar Harbor sold summers."}, {"lat": 44.3236, "lon": -68.323, "d": 6, "az": 110, "el": 15, "cap": "Acadia Mountain and Saint Sauveur, over the sound's mouth. On the earlier printings they are Robinson and Dog Mountain; Saint Sauveur remembers the French Jesuit mission planted at Fernald Point in 1613 and destroyed by an English raider the same summer."}]}, {"id": "park", "name": "Sieur de Monts", "blurb": "A monument in 1916, Lafayette National Park in 1919, Acadia in 1929 \u2014 three names on one plate, and the slider walks between them.", "keys": [{"lat": 44.3614, "lon": -68.2085, "d": 4, "az": 160, "el": 14, "cap": "Sieur de Monts Spring. George B. Dorr bought this spring and the ground around it and gave it away; on 8 July 1916 Woodrow Wilson proclaimed the whole holding Sieur de Monts National Monument \u2014 about five thousand acres, mostly bought a lot at a time by Dorr and Charles W. Eliot's trustees."}, {"lat": 44.3314, "lon": -68.2553, "d": 6, "az": 200, "el": 14, "cap": "Jordan Pond, under the Bubbles. The 1942 sheet draws the carriage roads John D. Rockefeller Jr. paid for between 1913 and 1940 \u2014 some forty-five miles of broken-stone road inside the park, built deliberately for horses and closed to cars."}, {"lat": 44.3204, "lon": -68.1878, "d": 5, "az": 300, "el": 13, "cap": "Thunder Hole and the Otter Cliffs. A Navy receiving station stood on the cliff top from 1917, for a few years one of the most important wireless ears on the Atlantic coast; in 1935 it was moved across the bay to Schoodic Point to clear the headland for the shore road."}, {"lat": 44.3507, "lon": -68.0529, "d": 7, "az": 310, "el": 14, "cap": "Schoodic Head, across the bay. The peninsula came to the park in 1929 from the heirs of John G. Moore; the family would not give land to a park named for a Frenchman, so on 19 January 1929 Lafayette National Park became Acadia. The primary sheet still says Lafayette."}]}, {"id": "pemetic", "name": "Pemetic", "blurb": "Wabanaki country. The island had a name, a language and five thousand years of shell heaps before Champlain sailed past it and wrote down another.", "keys": [{"lat": 44.4142, "lon": -68.185, "d": 10, "az": 250, "el": 14, "cap": "Frenchman Bay. This is Wabanaki homeland \u2014 Penobscot and Passamaquoddy, with Maliseet and Mi'kmaq relations. Their name for the island is Pemetic, the sloping land. Champlain struck a ledge off its east shore on 5 September 1604, saw the bare granite summits, and wrote down l'Isle des Monts-d\u00e9serts."}, {"lat": 44.2959, "lon": -68.312, "d": 5, "az": 30, "el": 13, "cap": "Fernald Point, at the mouth of Somes Sound. Shell heaps here and around the shore hold oyster and clam middens going back some five thousand years \u2014 the plainest record of who lived on this coast, and it is written in the ground, not on the plate."}, {"lat": 44.3876, "lon": -68.2039, "d": 5, "az": 140, "el": 14, "cap": "The Bar Harbor shore. Through the resort decades Penobscot and Passamaquoddy families camped on the waterfront each summer selling ash baskets, canoes and moccasins to the cottagers; the town moved the encampment repeatedly and finally off the shore altogether. No edition of this sheet marks it."}, {"lat": 44.262, "lon": -68.2339, "d": 6, "az": 330, "el": 13, "cap": "Islesford, on Little Cranberry. Maine took Wabanaki land without a ratified federal treaty; the Passamaquoddy and Penobscot land claims over that failure ran until the Maine Indian Claims Settlement Act of 1980."}]}], "mines": [{"n": "Alley Island", "u": 0.2685, "v": 0.39577, "c": "island"}, {"n": "Ash Island", "u": 0.67186, "v": 0.18336, "c": "island"}, {"n": "Bald Porcupine Island", "u": 0.61733, "v": 0.4622, "c": "island"}, {"n": "Bald Rock", "u": 0.56698, "v": 0.35593, "c": "island"}, {"n": "Bar Island", "u": 0.57865, "v": 0.42563, "c": "island"}, {"n": "Bartlett Island", "u": 0.18717, "v": 0.57769, "c": "island"}, {"n": "Bean Island", "u": 0.57436, "v": 0.18338, "c": "island"}, {"n": "Bear Island", "u": 0.46975, "v": 0.78695, "c": "island"}, {"n": "Big Moose Island", "u": 0.81605, "v": 0.62038, "c": "island"}, {"n": "Birch Island", "u": 0.20015, "v": 0.61694, "c": "island"}, {"n"